{"records":[{"id":"jgd4x76t9c","title":"Graded oligodendrocyte vulnerability is regulated by the  transcription factor DNA-damage inducible transcript 3 (DDIT3)","description":"Oligodendrocytes are integral to efficient neuronal signaling. Loss of myelinating oligodendrocytes is a central feature of many neurological diseases, among multiple sclerosis. Results of neuropathological studies suggest that oligodendrocytes react with differing sensitivity to toxic insults, with some cells dying early during lesion development and some cells being resistant for even weeks. Such a proposed graded vulnerability has never been demonstrated but provides an attractive window for therapeutic interventions. Beyond, biochemical pathways associated with graded oligodendrocyte vulnerability are not well explored. \nBy immunohistochemistry and serial block-face scanning electron microscopy (3D-EM) we show that cuprizone-induced metabolic stress results in an “out of phase” degeneration of oligodendrocytes. While the expression induction of stress response transcriptions factors, specifically in oligodendrocytes, occurs within days, subsequent oligodendrocyte apoptosis is a process going on for weeks. In line with the idea of an out of phase degeneration of oligodendrocytes, detailed ultrastructural reconstructions of the axon-myelin unit demonstrate demyelination of single internodes. Genome wide array analysis revealed an active unfolded protein response early after initiation of cuprizone intoxication. Besides cytoprotective pathways, the pro-apoptotic transcription factor DNA-damage-inducible transcript 3 (DDIT3) was induced selectively in the vulnerable oligodendrocyte population. Besides the amelioration of toxin-induced oligodendrocyte apoptosis, demyelination, microgliosis, astrocytosis and acute axonal damage were less intense in Ddit3-null mutants. \nThis study identifies activation of an endoplasmic reticulum stress cascade in oligodendrocytes as an important regulator of a graded vulnerability of this cell population. Interference with such endoplasmic reticulum stress cascades offers a promising therapeutic approach for demyelinating disorders. \n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-03-26T12:00:24.672Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/jgd4x76t9c","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/jgd4x76t9c"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Markus","last_name":"Kipp","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Health Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"f7btcr6x6d","title":"A non-canonical function of glutathione to suppress ferroptosis via FSP1","description":"Whole genome-wide CRISPR KO screen","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-10-02T11:41:52.925Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/f7btcr6x6d","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/f7btcr6x6d"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"BO","last_name":"CHU","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"5zfkhtp9md","title":"Data for: Tunicamycin-induced endoplasmic reticulum stress up-regulates tumour-promoting cytokines in oral squamous cell carcinoma","description":"Detailed data tables of the paper: Tunicamycin-induced endoplasmic reticulum stress up-regulates tumour-promoting cytokines in oral squamous cell carcinoma.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-05-06T11:15:51.917Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/5zfkhtp9md","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/5zfkhtp9md"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Muhammed","last_name":"Yakin","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Dentistry","Pathology","Cell Signaling","Oral Carcinoma","Endoplasmic Reticulum Stress"],"journal_issn":["10434666"]},{"id":"bkxfv6yzn5","title":"ARMH3-mediated recruitment of Phosphatidylinositol 4-kinase beta directs Golgi-to-endosome trafficking and activation of the antiviral effector STING","description":"The cGAS-STING pathway mediates cytoplasmic DNA-triggered innate immunity. STING activation is initiated by cyclic-GMP-AMP (cGAMP)-induced translocation from the endoplasmic reticulum and sulfated glycosaminoglycans-induced polymerization at the Golgi. Here we examined the mechanisms underlying STING transport and activation beyond the Golgi. A genome-wide CRISPR–Cas9 screen identified Armadillo-like helical domain-containing protein 3 (ARMH3) as critical for STING activation. Upon cGAMP-triggered translocation, ARMH3 interacted with STING at the Golgi and recruited phosphatidylinositol 4-kinase beta (PI4KB) to synthesize PI4P, which directed STING Golgi-to-endosome trafficking via PI4P-binding proteins AP-1 and GGA2. Disrupting PI4P-dependent lipid transport through RNAi of other PI4P-binding proteins impaired STING activation. Consistently, disturbed lipid composition inhibited STING activation whereas aberrantly elevated cellular PI4P led to cGAS-independent STING activation. Armh3fl/fllLyzCre/Cre mice were susceptible to DNA virus challenge in vivo. Thus, ARMH3 bridges STING and PIK4B to generate PI4P for STING transportation and activation, an interaction conserved in all eukaryotes.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-02-20T13:53:07.979Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/bkxfv6yzn5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/bkxfv6yzn5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zhengfan","last_name":"Jiang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"run","last_name":"fang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Qifei","last_name":"Jiang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xinying","last_name":"Jia","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virus","Cytokines","Phosphoinositides","Innate Immunity"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rj74h4tp3f","title":"Han et al 2018 Cell Reports","description":"The data are the original, uncropped western blots in the paper entitiled \"FXR Inhibits Endoplasmic Reticulum Stress-Induced NLRP3 Inflammasome in Hepatocytes and Ameliorates Liver Injury\". ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-09-10T12:42:42.856Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rj74h4tp3f","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rj74h4tp3f"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Sang Geon","last_name":"Kim","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chang Yeob","last_name":"Han","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hyun Soo ","last_name":"Rho","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Hepatology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"zhpg8xshp9","title":"Lawrence et al., PNAS 2026","description":"Data from “Lawrence et al., 2026 A Genome-Wide Screen Identifies that PLCG2 Restrains Lysosomal GCase Activity.\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-06-22T18:23:16.389Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zhpg8xshp9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zhpg8xshp9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Baris","last_name":"Bingol","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Natural Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nxvdzybwhf","title":"Illuminating host-mycobacterial interactions with genome-wide CRISPR knockout and CRISPRi screens","description":"The data deposited here contains genome-wide CRISPR knockout and CRISPRi screen data, including the positive hits, the negative hits, and non-targeting controls. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-08-30T11:32:01.108Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nxvdzybwhf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nxvdzybwhf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yong","last_name":"Lai","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Functional Genomics","High-Throughput Sequencing","Tuberculosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"y8dndzn3mg","title":"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication","description":"These data are part of the Supplemental Items associated with the manuscript entitled \"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication\". These files include Supplementary Data file 1-4.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-03-16T10:34:13.579Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/y8dndzn3mg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/y8dndzn3mg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Limeng","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virus","Screening","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"bwtn6y3f96","title":"Identifying endoplasmic reticulum stress-related key genes of vascular dementia based on bioinformatics and biological experiments","description":"Identifying endoplasmic reticulum stress-related key genes of vascular dementia based on bioinformatics and biological experiments","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-09-09T19:38:16.456Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/bwtn6y3f96","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/bwtn6y3f96"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"yilong","last_name":"zhao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Endoplasmic Reticulum Stress","Vascular Dementia"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"h395cjfk4j","title":"Identifying endoplasmic reticulum stress-related key genes of vascular dementia using bioinformatics and biological experiments","description":"Identifying endoplasmic reticulum stress-related key genes of vascular dementia using bioinformatics and biological experiments","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","nam